<section class="reading-section" id="notes">
  <div class="reading-label"><p class="eyebrow">About the model</p><h2>The rules change<br />the answer.</h2></div>
  <div class="reading-copy">
    <p>Workers keep their vote after losing a job. They vote for the policy that improves their own expected income, including the cost of weaker investment. “Mathematicians first” describes the thought experiment; the model does not predict which profession comes next.</p>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>One ballot and international competition</summary>
      <p id="voting-rules">Every adult US citizen has one equal vote. Every combination in the policy menu is a complete package on one ballot: AI pace, job retention, benefit amounts, benefit formula and both tax rates, plus free trade in international mode. Each person chooses the fully funded package that gives their household the highest expected income utility. By default, the package with the most votes passes only if it has more than 50% of all votes; otherwise current taxes, benefits and AI pace remain. Checking “Retaining status quo isn’t an option” removes the exact current-policy package from the US ballot. The remaining fully funded package with the most votes wins regardless of its share. Everyone knows this rule. There is no runoff or second vote.</p>
      <p>These voters are perfectly rational and self-interested. They understand all modeled consequences over ten years, including losses from weaker investment, and give no separate value to anyone else’s welfare. Survey weights represent equal votes across the adult citizen population; income is never a voting weight. A person keeps their vote after losing a job. Job retention has no separate value beyond its income.</p>
      <p>The ballot rule requires each person to select their own favorite package. Exact income-utility ties prefer eligible current policy, then a fixed policy-ID ordering. When the most-votes rule applies, tied vote totals use that same ordering. With that rule, the winner must differ from current policy, which is neither on the US ballot nor an automatic fallback. This is an explicit voting assumption: rationality alone does not uniquely determine how voters coordinate or vote strategically.</p>
      <p>In international mode, the rest of the world is one actor. It chooses the fully funded package maximizing the objective you select, given the enacted US policy and the known US voting rule. US voters choose their favorite packages given that foreign policy. Removing US current policy from the ballot does not restrict the foreign actor’s menu: it can still choose its own current policy if fully funded. Every policy term can differ between the two economies. The calculator checks that the US ballot outcome holds given the foreign choice and that the foreign actor cannot improve its objective by changing its own package while US policy stays fixed. It reports an unverified result if that search fails. This does not establish that a consistent pair is unique, or that none exists when the search fails.</p>
      <p>The decisions are simultaneous and independent, with each side correctly anticipating the other’s choice. There is no joint objective, negotiation or agreement to match policies. Each policy stays in place for ten years. If both independently choose to pause AI, each expects the other’s pause to last the decade. The solver’s response steps check those expectations; they are not successive votes or opportunities to change a policy after seeing the other’s vote.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>The terms on the ballot</summary>
      <p><strong>1. AI pace.</strong> Pause AI, allow current AI pace, or accelerate AI. Checking “Pausing AI isn’t possible” removes Pause AI from all players’ choices. A pause prevents new AI exposure and AI job replacement in that economy for ten years. Current pace reaches full deployment in year ten; acceleration runs twice as fast and reaches it in year five. Full annual productivity gains arrive sooner on the accelerated path. If trade is open, cheaper foreign production can still displace workers even during a domestic AI pause.</p>
      <p><strong>2. Job retention.</strong> Employers can lay off workers or retain affected workers at 50%, 100% or 125% of their starting wage before tax. Paying someone to stay in a role that AI has replaced adds no output. The employer bears the cost, which reduces capital income. Retained workers can move into available productive roles before outside hires; they do not compete for jobs while on the protected payroll.</p>
      <p><strong>3. Benefit amounts.</strong> Government spending on the modeled benefits can be 0%, 50%, 100%, 150% or 200% of its survey baseline. Higher total spending does not mean every recipient receives more.</p>
      <p><strong>4. The benefit formula.</strong> Keep the current mix, pay every adult an equal amount, or link payments to prior income. “Prior income” means observed disposable income before AI. This reference stays fixed even if later job competition lowers wages. The current mix preserves the starting distribution of benefits; losing a job does not automatically create a new unemployment-benefit payment.</p>
      <p><strong>5. Non-capital taxes.</strong> The result compares the selected tax benchmark with the survey-based US reference and shows the actual average after incomes change. A lower rate is a tax reduction. The calculation retains differences in tax burdens across income groups.</p>
      <p><strong>6. Capital taxes.</strong> The same comparison applies to capital income. These are modeled average burdens, not statutory brackets or official separate rates for “workers” and “capital owners.” A person can receive both labor and capital income.</p>
      <p><strong>7. Free trade, in international mode.</strong> Each side allows or bans trade with the other economy. Trade occurs only if both allow it. A unilateral ban closes the border for both sides; it does not stop trade within the foreign bloc.</p>
      <p>A package is eligible only if it fully funds promised retained wages, benefits and other required public spending in every year, after economic responses. No borrowing is modeled. Under the default majority rule, current policy is the automatic fallback if no eligible package wins a majority, even when it becomes underfunded; any shortfall is shown explicitly. With the most-votes rule, an underfunded package cannot take office. If no available package is fully funded, the calculator reports that no result is available. Surplus receipts fund non-transfer public spending.</p>
      <p>The tax reference covers modeled federal and state income taxes after credits, plus payroll taxes including self-employment contributions. It excludes sales and property taxes, corporate taxes and employers’ contributions for employees. Modeled net tax refunds count as benefits; this does not capture the full value of every refundable credit. All combinations in this discrete menu are evaluated. It does not cover every possible percentage.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>Economic assumptions and limits</summary>
      <p>The US population and income distribution come from the Census survey. Household income is pooled and divided among adult household members. The model groups citizens by that household’s main income source, income level and their own employment status. Main sources include work, capital, public benefits and pensions. Benefit receipt and employment overlap. These group labels do not determine how anyone votes; their own projected income does.</p>
      <p>International defaults use relative GDP and bilateral trade exposure. The foreign actor uses the same income distribution as a simplifying assumption; it is not a survey of foreign voters. AI gains, job counts, job-search participation and responses to taxes remain adjustable assumptions. Zero foreign frontier capability leaves the foreign economy and its imports in place.</p>
      <p><strong>AI adds to annual growth.</strong> The default AI contribution is 5 percentage points at full exposure, on top of assumed background growth of 2% in the US and 3% abroad. During rollout, the contribution scales with that year’s AI exposure. Potential output compounds each year; a pause leaves background growth in place. Both background rates are illustrative assumptions informed by economic forecasts. Taxes, weaker investment and trade losses can outweigh the AI contribution and make the economy shrink.</p>
      <p>The employer-gain parameter divides that output. At a 50% gain, an automated role with a $100,000 prior wage gives the employer a $150,000 income claim. Keeping the employee at $100,000 leaves $50,000 before other adjustments. To keep total income consistent with the separate GDP assumption, all gross income claims are scaled to the available output before costs, taxes and retained wages are paid. This allocation rule can change workers’ and owners’ shares without changing the GDP path for a fixed policy. Changing those shares can change the policy voters choose.</p>
      <p><strong>Jobs affected and jobs lost are different.</strong> The defaults assume AI eventually changes every current job and reduces the total number of jobs by 90%. The affected share cannot be smaller than the net reduction: a 90% fall in jobs requires at least 90% of current jobs to be affected. Jobs can also change while their total stays constant, or while new jobs are added. Current AI pace reaches full domestic deployment by year ten; accelerated AI reaches it by year five.</p>
      <p><strong>Searching does not guarantee a job.</strong> The job-search setting is the share of newly laid-off workers who enter the pool of active jobseekers. They compete for the available jobs; there is no automatic annual return-to-work rate. The model keeps the starting workforce fixed, so additional jobs can remain vacant. It does not bring in immigrants or adults who had no work income at the start.</p>
      <p><strong>Competition changes pay through hiring.</strong> New contracts get a wage factor equal to available jobs divided by productive workers, active jobseekers and retained workers available for redeployment, capped at one. Existing contracts keep their factor until replacement. Actual pay can still change with output, work incentives and prices. When layoffs are allowed, one replacement round each year lets a jobseeker take an incumbent’s job at the lower factor; the newly laid-off incumbent can then enter the search pool. The calculation uses average contract factors within each job pool. Retention pay and the prior-income benefit formula keep their pre-AI reference.</p>
      <p>Taxes and required employer pay can delay rollout within the chosen five- or ten-year schedule and reduce productive effort. They can also erode existing capital capacity through an assumed 5% annual renewal requirement. This rate and the hiring rules are modeling choices, not estimates from the cited data.</p>
      <p>This is a comparison within a policy menu, not a full model of the world economy. Observed household investment income is not total company profit or wealth. The employer-funding limit and production responses remain modeling assumptions. It solves relative producer prices, consumer prices and demand under assumed substitution rules, not a full system of sector or asset prices. It does not model debt, firms’ investment decisions, occupation-specific matching or a separate job-search decision for each person. It omits lobbying, monetary policy, AI safety and military outcomes. Changing the policy menu or voting procedure can change the result.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details" id="trade-assumptions"><summary>Trade, prices and foreign competition</summary>
      <p><strong>Each side can close the border.</strong> Free trade requires both choices to permit it. A ban stops bilateral goods and services trade, imported AI and the modeled cross-border AI profit payments. Trade among countries inside the foreign bloc continues. The US-only scenario does not apply these international effects.</p>
      <p><strong>Cheaper imports and competing firms.</strong> The model separates spending on goods and services that can cross the border from spending on local services. The tradable share is fixed at 40% in this precomputed edition. Within that share, buyers substitute between domestic and foreign production as relative prices change. Trade elasticity is fixed at 4, informed by trade research; it is not an estimate specific to AI. Both economies’ output and cross-border profit income enter the calculation of the relative producer price. Consumer prices then determine what each household’s income can buy.</p>
      <p><strong>Trade changes the pool of jobs.</strong> Increasing import competition or falling export sales can remove productive jobs, including during a domestic AI pause. The adjustment uses the preceding year’s market changes: one percentage point of job capacity per point of import-share growth or export-volume loss relative to prior available output, capped by the tradable share. Reversing those changes can restore capacity. This rule changes job availability independently of how many laid-off workers search. New jobseekers compete for the remaining jobs under the same hiring and wage rules. The coefficient is an assumption; the cited research supports persistent adjustment costs, not this numerical rule. Foreign production is not counted as domestic AI output.</p>
      <p><strong>Closing trade is costly.</strong> Losing foreign products raises the consumer price index; lost export sales also require workers to move to other activities. Employer-retention and government-benefit promises keep their reference purchasing power, so a higher price index raises the receipts needed to fund them. Household income in the results is adjusted for these prices. Physical output is reported separately.</p>
      <p><strong>A balanced reference.</strong> The observed 2025 US import and export totals differ. The model replaces them with one common bilateral flow—the geometric mean of imports and exports—because it does not model foreign borrowing or deficit finance. At the defaults this is about $3.868 trillion in each direction. It caps this flow at the available tradable share when assumptions make the reference infeasible. The trade model therefore uses observed scale but does not reproduce the US trade deficit.</p>
      <p>These are two broad economies with a common tradable share, rather than individual industries or countries. The model omits tariffs, supply chains, exchange-rate policy and new trade partners. A rise in purchasing power from cheaper imports is not counted again as extra physical GDP. The outcomes are conditional on these assumptions, not forecasts of trade policy.</p>
    </details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>Equations and calculation method</summary><p>Every selectable scenario is calculated locally in Python before publication. The site only loads saved results, with no server-side calculations. Older links outside this smaller grid are explicitly moved to supported choices.</p><div id="model-method"></div></details>
    <details class="method-details"><summary>Why the pirate game?</summary><p>The pirate puzzle asks who can win enough votes to divide a fixed treasure. Automation changes how much is produced and who receives it. This simulator keeps the voting question and adds production, employment and government payments.</p></details>
  </div>
